title: Move the git_cert_hostkey offsets behind a Git::Libgit2 probe binding
updated: 2026-08-25T14:02:41Z
---

Follow-up to #27. t/75-cert-hostkey-layout.t now measures the three
git_cert_hostkey offsets Remote.pm compiles in, but it measures them from
the header rather than from the loaded library, and it needs a C compiler
to do it (skips otherwise). Remote.pm still carries the constants.

The cleaner long-term design is the one fetch_options_prune_offset already
uses in Git::Libgit2: a probe binding that derives the offset from the
library at runtime, so Git::Native reads it instead of hardcoding it.
git_cert_hostkey has no *_init function, so the marker-scan trick does not
carry over - a binding would have to expose the offsets some other way
(a small C accessor compiled into the XS/FFI side of Git::Libgit2, or
constants generated at Git::Libgit2 build time from git2.h, which is where
Alien::Libgit2's cflags are already available).

Spans two distributions: Git::Libgit2 gains the binding and a test, then
Git::Native drops CERT_HOSTKEY_TYPE_OFFSET / _SHA1_OFFSET / _SHA256_OFFSET
and pins the Git::Libgit2 version. Sequencing matters - Git::Libgit2 has to
be released first (see the getty-perl-core cpanfile rule).

Not urgent: all three offsets are correct on the 1.5 floor and on 1.9.3,
and t/75 now goes red if that stops being true on a machine with a compiler.

Git::Libgit2 30bd8fd + Git::Native c7adc54; offsets ABI-derived in the binding, t/75 cross-checks offsetof and pins the constants gone.
